  "result": "I wrote the rule and its witnesses, but the gate never checked them. All three gate runs returned `SCOPE_VIOLATION`: the gate's scope check rejected files under `.runtime/tmp/…` and stopped before building or running anything.\n\nThose files are task-output files the runtime writes on every shell command, some of them from other sessions (LVMC-11-18-020, LVMC-11-43-010-B-o1). Each gate call adds another one, so running again can't get past the check. The fix belongs in the harness: its scope check should ignore `.runtime/tmp`. I stopped at 3 of 6 runs. The harness re-runs the gate after this session, and that run may get further.\n\nTwo of those runs were my mistakes. The first was the gate command with extra shell attached, sent before I had written anything. The second was a stray call, also before any edits. Only the third run was on the finished files.\n\n**The rule** (`library/TrafficSpec/Rules/LVMC_11/LVMC_11_30_050.lean`):\n- **Who it covers:** a member of `DEF-NRS-484A-165::pedestrian`.\n- **When it applies:** the pedestrian is on a roadway, in no crosswalk, and on a lane or roadway it is moving across within `p.crossing_motion_cos`.\n- **What it requires:** for every lane and roadway that meets that same test, the pedestrian moves across it within `p.right_angle_crossing_cos`.\n- I added no narrowings of my own.\n\n**The witnesses** (`library/TrafficSpecWitness/LVMC_11/LVMC_11_30_050.lean`): the five planned scenes plus a `branch_2`, in which a pedestrian crosses diagonally over a one-way roadway with no lanes. That makes the roadway itself the carrier, so the roadway case gets a test of its own. Headings are chosen with clear margins against the reading's values:\n\n| Heading | Cosine with the lane direction | Against the bounds |\n|---|---|---|\n| Diagonal, about 37° off perpendicular | 0.6 | counts as crossing (≤ 0.94), not at right angles (> 0.26) |\n| Near-square, about 5.5° off perpendicular | about 0.095 | at right angles (≤ 0.26) |\n\nTwo scenes depart slightly from the plan's wording:\n- **`permit`:** it uses a one-way roadway, not \"the same roadway\" as `forbid`. On a two-way roadway the roadway has no direction, which would leave that scene unknown instead of compliant.\n- **Angles:** the diagonal is about 37° rather than 40°, and the near-square about 5.5° rather than 5°, so the vectors stay exact rationals.\n\n`task_result.md` in the working directory has the run log and these choices.",
